			"id": "109864185443342444",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@justinfagnani/109864185402109088",
			"author": {
				"name": "Justin Fagnani",
				"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@justinfagnani",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/306/676/088/719/526/original/1402f1c70ff2119d.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2023-02-14T17:07:10.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Unfortunately they get the purpose of a few proposals I worked on around modules wrong.</p><p>The immediate goal of being able to import HTML and CSS into JS isn't to make JS the center of the universe, but to reduce the need for build steps to transform files into JS in the first place. It's *less* JS centric this way.</p><p>Then I think we can let HTML import non-JS modules directly. ie, HTML page imports HTML module with declarative custom element definitions and no actual JS involved.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "109864220025423003",
			"parentId": "109864185443342444",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/109864220025423003",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-02-14T17:15:58.889Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fosstodon.org/@justinfagnani\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>justinfagnani</span></a></span> hmm—a few thoughts:</p><p>1. Does this repeat the same platform defaults mistake that React made?</p><p>“We had SSR from the beginning!” they cry, but few folks used it that way for *years* because the oxygen was being consumed by client rendering patterns.</p><p>2. Personally, I don’t want to trade away a build step for more client JavaScript. I have no problem with build-only server-only JS being the center of the universe—as long as the output of that build is HTML-first.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "109864399479022436",
			"parentId": "109864220025423003",
			"url": "https://indieweb.social/@McNeely/109864399457794206",
			"author": {
				"name": "McNeely",
				"url": "https://indieweb.social/@McNeely",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/552/212/183/557/481/original/75a9716caf3885be.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-02-14T18:01:36.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fosstodon.org/@justinfagnani\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>justinfagnani</span></a></span> As I remember using HTML imports it wasn't particularly about reducing builds steps to zero, but to cut out a level of transpilation because the platform layers understand each other. You could just have HTML without needing a DSL like JSX and all of its compromises.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "109864671323091834",
			"parentId": "109864399479022436",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/109864671323091834",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-02-14T19:10:45.143Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://indieweb.social/@McNeely\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>McNeely</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fosstodon.org/@justinfagnani\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>justinfagnani</span></a></span> ah, when I hear HTML imports, I think of the ability to import HTML using HTML. If your usage here is referencing the ability to import HTML in JS that makes sense to me—and don’t get me wrong it sounds like a worthwhile goal!</p><p>But on a priority list I’d put the HTML to HTML feature higher</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "109864744198240086",
			"parentId": "109864671323091834",
			"url": "https://webperf.social/@andydavies/109864744105289868",
